The third toy tests one popular answer, an **ensemble**: train 5 policies on the same demonstrations from different random starts and resamples of the data, and treat their disagreement as uncertainty. The task is Week 9's: drive a point from the left of a table round a post in the middle to a goal on the right. Then test it four ways: on tasks like the training ones; mirrored, from right to left; turned, from bottom to top; and like the training ones but with the post moved 0\.3 upwards, something the policy cannot see, because the post's position is not one of its inputs.

::: {#cell-fig-introspection .cell execution_count=3}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Third toy: an ensemble of 5 cloned policies on 300 test tasks per condition (mean over 4 training seeds). Left: success rate of the ensemble's mean action. Right: disagreement between the members, averaged over each run.](w11-frontiers_files/figure-html/fig-introspection-output-1.png){#fig-introspection width=679 height=266 fig-alt='Two bar charts side by side over four conditions: as trained, mirrored, turned, post moved. Left, success: as trained about 0.98, mirrored about 0.07, turned about 0.33, post moved about 0.49. Right, disagreement: as trained and post moved both low, about 0.04, and exactly equal; mirrored and turned high, about 0.38 and 0.36.'}
:::
:::


The ensemble does well on what it can see (@fig-introspection). On mirrored and turned tasks its members disagree 10 and 10 times more than on familiar ones, and those are exactly the tasks where it fails (success 0\.07 and 0\.33). With the post moved, success falls to 0\.49, and the disagreement is *exactly* what it was on the familiar tasks: the inputs are the same, so every network computes the same outputs and drives into the post with full confidence. If the robot asks for help whenever disagreement exceeds what 99% of familiar tasks show, it asks on 100% of mirrored tasks and on 1\.0% of the moved-post ones. Disagreement measures how unfamiliar the *input* is, not whether the world still works as the data said, which is the lecture's warning about situations that look familiar.

::: {#cell-fig-riskcov .cell execution_count=4}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![Third toy: act on the share of tasks (x) with the lowest ensemble disagreement and hand the rest to a person; y is the success rate on the tasks the robot keeps. Blue: a mix of familiar, mirrored and turned tasks. Orange: the same mix plus the moved-post tasks.](w11-frontiers_files/figure-html/fig-riskcov-output-1.png){#fig-riskcov width=524 height=297 fig-alt='Line chart, x-axis share of tasks the robot acts on from 0 to 1, y-axis success on those tasks from 0 to 1. The blue line stays at 1 up to about a third of the tasks, then falls steadily to about 0.46 at full coverage. The orange line is noisy near zero coverage, then sits between 0.76 and 0.8 up to about 0.45 coverage, joins the blue line near 0.52 and ends near 0.47.'}
:::
:::


Ranked by disagreement, the robot can keep the familiar third of the first mix and succeed on 99% of it (@fig-riskcov). Add the moved-post tasks and no threshold separates them from the familiar ones: at half coverage success is only 74%. A useful introspection signal has to see the failure, which here would mean sensing the post or noticing contact.

### Imitation is bounded by its demonstrations {#stitching-intuition}

Most robot policies in this course were trained by imitation, and a cloned policy is bounded by its data [▶ 28:38](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=1718). The lecture's example: demonstrations go from state 1 to 2 and from 2 to 3. However large the model, cloning cannot learn to go from 1 to 3, let alone that a better route exists [▶ 29:10](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=1750). Offline RL can: it learns values for states from all the data and **stitches** sub-optimal trajectories into better ones [▶ 29:41](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=1781). Getting RL to work at scale on real robots is, in the lecturer's view, among the highest-impact problems to work on [▶ 30:45](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=1845).

The first toy builds that example in a small building: two rooms joined by an opening, and a corridor below reached through two doors. Operators walk between landmarks A to F, mostly straight but straying a quarter of the time. Behavior cloning is goal-conditioned with hindsight relabelling: every step that some trajectory took on its way to a later visit of the goal becomes training data for that goal. Offline RL, in a deterministic world with one unit of cost per step, reduces to the shortest path through the transitions the data contain.


```{=html}
<figure class="hero-video">
<video autoplay loop muted playsinline controls preload="metadata" poster="media/w11-stitch-poster.png" aria-label="Animation, about 20 seconds, looping. A grid map seen from above: grey walls around two rooms side by side, joined by an opening, above a corridor reached through two doors. Black discs mark A in the upper left room, B at the left end of the corridor and C at its right end. A blue line is drawn from A down through the left door to B, with a short stray step at the start, then a green line along the corridor from B to C. Both fade. An orange line grows from A and wanders in small loops around A in the upper left room; the caption reads &#x27;Cloning: 40 steps, never reaches C&#x27;. The orange fades and a thick black line goes from A down through the left door and along the corridor to C, joining the green line one cell before B: &#x27;Offline RL: A to C in 16 steps&#x27;. All lines fade back to the empty map." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:6px">
<source src="media/w11-stitch.mp4" type="video/mp4">
</video>
<figcaption>The lecture&#x27;s example in the first toy&#x27;s building: one demonstration from A to B and one from B to C. Asked to go from A to C, the cloned policy has no data and wanders; offline RL joins the two demonstrations.</figcaption>
</figure>
```



**What to watch for.** The map stays still; only the paths change. The data are an operator's walk from A to B (10 steps, with a stray step at the start) and another's from B to C (12 steps). Asked to go from A to C, the cloned policy finds no step in the data that was on the way to C from A, so it acts at random and circles near A until its 40 steps run out. Offline RL treats the data as a set of moves: from A it follows the first demonstration down through the door, switches to the second where they meet, drops the stray step, and arrives in 16 steps, which is also the shortest path in the building (16 steps). Neither demonstration does that; together they contain it.

::: {#cell-fig-stitch .cell execution_count=6}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![First toy: share of the 30 landmark-to-landmark tasks solved within 40 steps, against the number of operator trajectories (mean ± standard error over 8 seeds). Right: extra steps over the shortest path, on the tasks solved.](w11-frontiers_files/figure-html/fig-stitch-output-1.png){#fig-stitch width=679 height=266 fig-alt='Two line charts sharing an x-axis, number of operator trajectories on a log scale: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32. Left, tasks solved: behavior cloning (orange) rises from about 0.2 to 0.83; offline RL (blue) from about 0.23 to 1.0, above cloning from 4 trajectories on, by about 0.15 to 0.3. Right, extra steps over the shortest path: cloning falls from about 9 to 4.5; offline RL from about 11 at 2 trajectories to about 0.2 at 32.'}
:::
:::


With 8 trajectories, cloning solves 44% of the 30 tasks and offline RL 72%; with 32, 83% against 100% (@fig-stitch). Cloning can only repeat a route some operator took from here to the goal, stray steps included: even on the tasks it solves it takes 4\.5 extra steps at 32 trajectories, where offline RL takes 0\.2. Offline RL joins pieces of different trajectories and drops their detours, so it can beat every operator. With only 2 trajectories there is little to join, and the two are close (0\.19 and 0\.23). Where a policy has no data for its goal, both move at random, so a few of these successes are luck.

### The data flywheel, and why mixing data naively fails {#flywheel-intuition}

The holy grail is a **data flywheel**: better models make more capable robots, which get deployed more widely, which produce more data, which make better models [▶ 31:16](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=1876). The obvious way to start it is to co-train on the expert's demonstrations and the robot's own rollouts [▶ 31:47](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=1907). It is harder than it looks: expert data are smooth, dense and near-optimal; autonomous rollouts are longer, jerkier and often sub-optimal, and naively cloning both confuses the model [▶ 32:18](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=1938) [▶ 32:49](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=1969). New algorithms are needed that learn from all of a robot's experience, good or bad, and never throw data away [▶ 33:21](https://youtu.be/eL4lcy1KNzE?t=2001).

