## NumPy (runs on this page)

```python
def contrastive_losses(S, t=10.0, b=-10.0):
    """CLIP's softmax loss and SigLIP's sigmoid loss for a similarity
    matrix S (n, n) whose diagonal holds the matching pairs."""
    logits = t * S
    n = len(S)
    lsm_r = logits - np.log(np.exp(logits).sum(1, keepdims=True))   # image -> text
    lsm_c = logits - np.log(np.exp(logits).sum(0, keepdims=True))   # text -> image
    clip = -(np.trace(lsm_r) + np.trace(lsm_c)) / (2 * n)
    z = 2 * np.eye(n) - 1                                # +1 match, -1 not
    sig = np.log1p(np.exp(-z * (logits + b))).sum() / n
    return float(clip), float(sig)
```


## PyTorch (sketch)
```{.python .snippet}
import torch
import torch.nn.functional as F

torch.manual_seed(0)
n, d = 8, 16
img = F.normalize(torch.randn(n, d), dim=1)               # (n, d) unit vectors
txt = F.normalize(img + 0.3 * torch.randn(n, d), dim=1)   # matching captions
t, b = torch.tensor(10.0), torch.tensor(-10.0)            # learned in practice

logits = t * img @ txt.T                                  # (n, n); diagonal = matches
labels = torch.arange(n)
# CLIP: softmax over each row (image -> captions) and each column.
clip = (F.cross_entropy(logits, labels) + F.cross_entropy(logits.T, labels)) / 2
# SigLIP: an independent binary decision per pair, +1 on the diagonal.
z = 2 * torch.eye(n) - 1
siglip = -F.logsigmoid(z * (logits + b)).sum() / n

shuffled = txt[torch.randperm(n)]                         # break the pairing
clip_bad = F.cross_entropy(t * img @ shuffled.T, labels)
assert clip < clip_bad and siglip > 0
```
:::

`F.cross_entropy(logits, labels)` applies the softmax along `dim=1` (each row); passing `logits.T` does the columns. `F.logsigmoid` is $\log \sigma(\cdot)$ computed stably. In a real run the batch is split across many devices; CLIP must gather all embeddings to normalize each row, while SigLIP can process blocks of the matrix independently, which is why it scales to larger batches.

## Papers

### Papers discussed in class

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[A Path Towards Autonomous Machine Intelligence](https://openreview.net/pdf?id=BZ5a1r-kVsf)**<br>
LeCun · *position paper, OpenReview* 2022

A position paper on how machines could learn as efficiently as animals: mostly by observing, with a predictive world model, and acting either reactively or by planning through that model. It proposes a modular architecture whose world model predicts in an abstract representation space (JEPA) rather than in pixels.

- Six modules: configurator, perception, world model, cost (an intrinsic cost plus a trainable critic), short-term memory and actor; all differentiable (Sec. 3, Fig. 2).
- Two modes of acting, after Kahneman: Mode-1 reacts directly; Mode-2 plans by optimizing actions through the world model against the cost, like model-predictive control (Sec. 3.1).
- The Joint Embedding Predictive Architecture: predict the representation of y from that of x, so details that cannot be predicted need not be modelled; trained non-contrastively (Sec. 4.4–4.5, Eqs. 10–12).
- Hierarchical JEPA for planning at several time scales, and the argument that neither scaling nor reward alone is enough (Sec. 4.6–4.7, 8.3).

**On this page:** Week 8's world models and JEPA, and on this page the 'when should a robot reason?' question: Mode-1 is the fast reactive policy, Mode-2 the slow deliberate one.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[The Bitter Lesson](http://www.incompleteideas.net/IncIdeas/BitterLesson.html)**<br>
Sutton · *essay* 2019

A one-page essay drawing the lesson of 70 years of AI research: general methods that exploit growing computation, search and learning, eventually beat methods that build in human knowledge of the domain, even though the knowledge-based methods win in the short term.

- The pattern in four fields: chess, Go, speech recognition and computer vision, where hand-built knowledge gave way to search and learning at scale.
- Why the lesson keeps being relearned: building in knowledge helps at first and satisfies the researcher, then plateaus and gets in the way.
- The prescription: build in the meta-methods that discover structure, not the structure itself.

**On this page:** The lecture's flywheel (more data, better models) and the 'train from scratch on enough robot data' camp in the backbone debate; the guest's history of vision, where scaling model, data and training time together won, is the lesson played out.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Intelligence without Representation](https://people.csail.mit.edu/brooks/papers/representation.pdf)**<br>
Brooks · *Artificial Intelligence* 1991

Argues that AI went wrong by decomposing intelligence into perception, a central world model and action connected by symbolic representations. Brooks instead builds robots ('Creatures') from layers of simple behaviours that each sense and act on the world directly, adding layers one at a time and testing each in the real world.

- A conclusion and a hypothesis: for simple intelligence, explicit world models get in the way, 'better to use the world as its own model'; and representation is the wrong unit of abstraction for building most of an intelligent system (Sec. 1).
- The evolutionary argument: evolution spent billions of years on moving and sensing and only a few million on language and reasoning, so the former is the hard part (Sec. 2).
- Decomposition by activity, not function: parallel layers that each produce behaviour, built and debugged incrementally on robots running in ordinary offices (Sec. 4–6).

**On this page:** The lecture's case that foundation models lack physical grounding, and that embodied data and real-world testing are what close the gap; also the end-to-end learning the lecturer chose over a pipeline of separate modules.
:::

### Key papers from the lecture

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Beyond Sight: Finetuning Generalist Robot Policies with Heterogeneous Sensors via Language Grounding](https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.04693)**<br>
Jones, Mees, Sferrazza, Stachowicz, Abbeel & Levine · *ICRA* 2025

FuSe fine-tunes a generalist robot policy, trained on vision and proprioception only, to use touch and sound as well, though no large data sets of those senses exist. Language is the common ground: the new senses are tied to descriptions of what the robot sees, feels and hears.

- A multimodal contrastive loss plus a language-generation loss grounded in the senses, used during fine-tuning (Abstract).
- New zero-shot abilities such as prompting with several senses at once and describing an object the robot is touching (Abstract).
- Works for both a diffusion-head policy and a large VLA; over 20% higher success than the baselines in real-world tests (Abstract).

**On this page:** The lecture's 'long tail of robot sensing': scarce, unpaired, heterogeneous touch and audio data, bridged with language.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Visual Language Maps for Robot Navigation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.05714)**<br>
Huang, Mees, Zeng & Burgard · *ICRA* 2023

VLMaps fuses features from a pretrained vision-language model into a 3-D reconstruction of the environment, built from the robot's own video while it explores. The map can then be queried in language, so an LLM can turn instructions like 'three meters to the right of the chair' into navigation goals.

- A spatial map whose cells carry open-vocabulary visual-language features, with no extra labelled data (Abstract).
- Spatial goals by construction: language commands become sequences of goals located in the map (Abstract).
- One map shared by robots with different bodies, each deriving its own obstacle map (Abstract).

**On this page:** The lecture's question of how a mobile robot should represent a building: an explicit map enriched with semantics, one end of the spectrum that runs to a walk-through video in the model's context.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[LeLaN: Learning a Language-Conditioned Navigation Policy from In-the-Wild Video](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03603)**<br>
Hirose, Glossop, Sridhar, Mees & Levine · *CoRL* 2024

Learns to navigate to objects described in language from videos that carry no actions: robot recordings, YouTube tours and people walking. Vision-language and robot foundation models label the videos with instructions and actions, and a small policy is trained on the result.

- Labelling unlabelled, action-free egocentric video with object instructions and actions using foundation models (Abstract).
- A policy that beats state-of-the-art navigation methods in over 1,000 real trials while running 4× faster on edge hardware (Abstract, arXiv v1).

**On this page:** Mapless navigation learned from internet video, the other end of the map question, and the lecture's point that performance kept rising with more YouTube data.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[CALVIN: A Benchmark for Language-Conditioned Policy Learning for Long-Horizon Robot Manipulation Tasks](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03227)**<br>
Mees, Hermann, Rosete-Beas & Burgard · *RA-L* 2022

An open simulated benchmark for robots that follow chains of language instructions over long horizons, from onboard sensors, generalizing zero-shot to new instructions and new scenes.

- Long-horizon, language-conditioned manipulation tasks with flexible sensor suites (Abstract).
- Zero-shot evaluation on novel instructions and environments (Abstract).
- A multicontext imitation baseline that performs poorly, leaving room for research (Abstract).

**On this page:** The lecturer's own story on this page: a benchmark that began as a by-product of the simulator he built to iterate faster.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Big Transfer (BiT): General Visual Representation Learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.11370)**<br>
Kolesnikov, Beyer, Zhai, Puigcerver, Yung, Gelly & Houlsby · *ECCV* 2020

Revisits supervised pre-training on large labelled data sets followed by fine-tuning, scales it up, and distils it into a simple recipe that transfers well from one example per class to a million examples.

- A few carefully chosen components and a simple heuristic for setting transfer hyperparameters (Abstract).
- 87.5% on ImageNet, 99.4% on CIFAR-10 and 76.3% on the 19-task VTAB (Abstract).
- Strong few-shot transfer: 76.8% on ImageNet with 10 examples per class (Abstract).

**On this page:** The guest's lesson that large-scale pre-training only pays off when model size, data and training time all grow together.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision](https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.00020)**<br>
Radford et al. · *ICML* 2021

CLIP trains an image encoder and a text encoder together to predict which caption goes with which image, on 400 million image-text pairs from the internet. Language then names any visual concept, so the model classifies images zero-shot.

- A contrastive objective over a batch: match each image to its own caption among all captions in the batch, and vice versa (Abstract, Sec. 2).
- Zero-shot transfer to over 30 vision data sets; zero-shot ImageNet accuracy matching the original ResNet-50 without its 1.28 million training examples (Abstract).

**On this page:** The guest's turning point: free-form web text instead of a fixed list of classes. This page's worked examples compute its loss and the batch shortcut that keeps it from learning relations.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Sigmoid Loss for Language Image Pre-Training](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15343)**<br>
Zhai, Mustafa, Kolesnikov & Beyer · *ICCV* 2023

Replaces CLIP's softmax over the whole batch with an independent yes/no decision for every image-text pair. The loss needs no global normalization, so it scales to larger batches and also works better at small ones.

- A pairwise sigmoid loss on image-text pairs (Abstract).
- 84.5% zero-shot ImageNet accuracy with locked-image tuning, trained in two days on four TPUv4 chips (Abstract).
- A study of batch size up to one million and of the ratio of negatives to positives (Abstract).

**On this page:** The vision encoder of PaliGemma, and so of many VLAs on this course's earlier pages.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[Image Captioners Are Scalable Vision Learners Too](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07915)**<br>
Tschannen, Kumar, Steiner, Zhai, Houlsby & Beyer · *NeurIPS* 2023

A careful, matched comparison of contrastive pre-training and plain image captioning on the same web data, compute and model size. Captioning, long thought inferior, turns out competitive for classification and better for vision-and-language tasks.

- Captioning produces vision encoders competitive with contrastive ones on classification and better on vision-language tasks (Abstract).
- Captioning scales as well as or better than contrastive training with architecture, model size and data (Abstract).

**On this page:** The guest's fix for CLIP's blind spot: to predict the word after 'sitting', a captioner must learn 'left of' versus 'right of'.
:::

::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon=false}
**[PaliGemma: A Versatile 3B VLM for Transfer](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.07726)**<br>
Beyer, Steiner, Pinto, Kolesnikov, Wang et al. · *arXiv* 2024

An open 3-billion-parameter vision-language model, a SigLIP image encoder joined to the Gemma-2B language model, trained to be a base that transfers well rather than a chat model.

- SigLIP-So400m image encoder plus Gemma-2B, trained in stages: unimodal pre-training, multimodal pre-training, higher resolution, then transfer (Sec. 3.2).
- Evaluated on almost 40 diverse tasks, including remote sensing and segmentation (Abstract).

**On this page:** The backbone of many VLAs (Week 9), and the guest's example of mid-training: stitching two pre-trained models together and teaching them new skills such as reading text and locating objects.
:::



## Guest spotlight

**Lucas Beyer** (now at Meta; earlier at Google Brain and DeepMind in Zürich, co-author of the Vision Transformer, SigLIP and PaliGemma, the VLM behind many VLAs) gave, as he put it, the least robotic lecture of the course: how vision went from not working to working, before anyone turns it into a VLA [▶ 01:33](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=93). His long-term goal is a robot any non-technical person can teach to do almost anything; eight years ago that needed robust, general perception and learning from few examples, and perception was the part that did not work [▶ 03:37](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=217) [▶ 04:08](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=248). People learn a new visual category from a handful of examples; his team made that the target, as a benchmark of many diverse tasks with few examples each (VTAB) [▶ 05:11](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=311) [▶ 06:46](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=406). Today's answer is a recipe: pre-train, mid-train, fine-tune, then RL-tune [▶ 08:18](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=498).

**Pre-training.** Self-supervised methods were all over the place, with no correlation between how well a model did its pre-training task and how well it transferred; only making the model larger helped consistently [▶ 09:50](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=590) [▶ 10:52](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=652). Adding a few labelled examples to pre-training changed that, and then large-scale supervised pre-training (BiT) beat everything published, also on deliberately weird test images [▶ 11:57](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=717) [▶ 15:06](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=906). Why it took the field so long: ten times more data alone made results *worse*; it takes larger models, more data and longer training together, patience through curves that look flat for eight GPU-weeks, and details such as weight decay that only pay off late [▶ 17:40](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1060) [▶ 18:13](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1093) [▶ 18:43](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1123). Labelled data sets are bounded by someone's list of classes (COCO's 80 classes famously came from a child naming things); web images with their alt text are not, which CLIP exploited and SigLIP simplified [▶ 20:47](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1247) [▶ 23:24](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1404). He argued against filtering that data down to English and ImageNet-like images, which made models worse on the rest of the world [▶ 25:28](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1528) [▶ 27:36](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1656). Contrastive training's blind spot is relations ([Example 2](#ex-batch)), fixed simply by also captioning [▶ 29:46](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1786) [▶ 31:50](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1910). And his main lesson: **look at the data**. A benchmark said to test relations could be solved without the image ("the horse is eating the grass" or "the grass is eating the horse"), as a blind captioner proved [▶ 32:51](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=1971).

**Mid-training and fine-tuning.** A VLM such as PaliGemma joins a pretrained image encoder to a language model and teaches the pair core skills with a broad mix of tasks: captioning, reading text, finding and segmenting objects, often at increasing image resolution; detection boxes and segmentation masks become tokens the language model can write [▶ 34:26](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=2066) [▶ 37:04](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=2224) [▶ 39:38](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=2378). Papers claiming VLMs are "blind" test tasks no one put in the fine-tuning mix: with 128 generated examples of a line-crossing task, PaliGemma reached 95–96% [▶ 42:19](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=2539) [▶ 43:20](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=2600). His provocative conclusion is that perception is solved, as a recipe: collect a few hundred examples of your task in an afternoon and fine-tune a pretrained model; reasoning and planning are what come next [▶ 44:24](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=2664). In the Q&A: don't pre-train small, fine-tune instead [▶ 47:29](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=2849); augmentation is worth at most about one order of magnitude of data [▶ 50:04](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=3004); remove near-duplicates of test images from pre-training, or you are not measuring what you think [▶ 50:36](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=3036); don't freeze the image encoder inside a VLM [▶ 52:38](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=3158); and distil large models into fast ones for robots [▶ 1:02:03](https://youtu.be/0XB7fNS_ONg?t=3723).

**How it connects to this week.** His history is the Bitter Lesson played out in vision, and his "look at the data" is this page's introspection toy in miniature: a number (disagreement, a benchmark score) can look fine while measuring something else. The open problems the lecture lists begin where his recipe stops: reasoning, planning and action.

## Solved problems {#problems}

**1. Stitching by hand.** The data hold three trajectories: 1 → 2 → 3, 4 → 2 → 5 and 5 → 6. Which of the tasks 1 → 3, 1 → 5, 1 → 6 and 4 → 3 can goal-conditioned cloning with relabelling solve, and which can offline RL solve?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Cloning needs one trajectory that visits the start and later the goal (@eq-relabel): only 1 → 3 (trajectory one). Offline RL works on the graph of observed transitions 1→2, 2→3, 4→2, 2→5, 5→6 (@eq-bellman-data): 1 → 3 directly, 1 → 5 via state 2 (stitching trajectories one and two), 1 → 6 via 2 and 5 (all three), and 4 → 3 via 2. So offline RL solves all four, cloning one.
:::

**2. Diluting the expert.** You have 50 expert episodes and deploy the robot 200 times per week, adding every rollout to a plain cloning data set. After how many weeks is the expert less than 10% of the data? What two changes would stop the policy from imitating itself?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
$50/(50 + 200k) < 0.1 \iff 200k > 450 \iff k > 2.25$, so after 3 weeks (@eq-share). Fixes: weight data by quality, for instance by advantage (Week 9's π\*0.6) or by success, or use offline RL, which uses bad rollouts to learn what *not* to do instead of copying them; and keep a fixed share of expert data in every batch regardless of its size.
:::

**3. A threshold that cannot work.** An ensemble's disagreement is identical on two sets of tasks, but the robot succeeds on 98% of one and 50% of the other, mixed in equal numbers. What is the best success you can get on the tasks you act on, and what would you need to do better?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
Any threshold accepts the same share of each set, so selective success is $(0.98 + 0.5)/2 = 0.74$ at every coverage (@eq-selective, the orange line of @fig-riskcov near its start). To do better you need a signal that differs between the sets: a sensor that sees the changed world (here, the post), feedback during execution such as contact or lack of progress, or data from the changed world so the ensemble disagrees there.
:::

**4. Batch shortcuts.** A contrastive model trains with batches of 4,096. One image in a thousand shows two people shaking hands; one in a million shows a particular pair of people shaking hands in a mirrored arrangement. How often does a batch contain a second handshake picture, and how often the mirrored scene?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
@eq-hardneg: $1 - 0.999^{4095} \approx 1 - e^{-4.10} \approx 0.98$; $1 - (1 - 10^{-6})^{4095} \approx 4.1 \times 10^{-3}$. The model will learn "handshake" but almost never needs the arrangement. A captioning loss, or a deliberately constructed hard negative, is needed for that.
:::

**5. Be your own reviewer two.** Your idea: fine-tune a VLA on 50 demonstrations per task on your lab's robot and claim it generalizes to unseen kitchens. Apply the lecture's checklist: what is the most likely reason this fails as a research contribution, and how would you change it?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Solution"}
The most likely failure is the lecture's "rockstar demo": data collected, trained and tested in one setting, so any success is interpolation and the claim of generalization is untested. Change the evaluation, not the method: hold out kitchens (or at least lighting, viewpoints and objects) entirely, report success with error bars over enough trials (Week 9's binomial standard error), compare against the same model without fine-tuning, and look at the failures. If the result survives, the next question is a problem-driven one: what limits generalization, and what single change fixes it?
:::

## Recap

1. **Generalization is the open problem.** Foundation models generalize without physical grounding; robot specialists are grounded but narrow. *Why it matters:* most impressive demos are interpolation, and the field's choices of backbone, data recipe and data collection are still open.
2. **Dexterity needs senses that data barely cover.** Touch and force are scarce, unpaired and heterogeneous; language can bind them. *Why it matters:* a robot blind at contact keeps crushing mugs.
3. **A robot must know what it doesn't know.** Adaptive reasoning and asking for help depend on it. Ensemble disagreement flags unfamiliar inputs, but in the toy it was blind to a moved obstacle, success 0\.49 at familiar-level disagreement. *Why it matters:* the failure that hurts is the one that looks familiar.
4. **Imitation is bounded by its demonstrations; RL can stitch and improve.** In the toy, offline RL solved 72% of the tasks from 8 trajectories against 44% for cloning. *Why it matters:* it is the way past human skill and past the routes people happened to demonstrate.
5. **The flywheel needs algorithms that learn from bad data.** Naively cloning a robot's own rollouts made the toy policy worse round after round; offline RL on the same data made it near-perfect. *Why it matters:* deployment data are the only data that scale with the number of robots.
6. **From the guest: scale model, data and training together, and look at the data.** *Why it matters:* it is how perception was "solved", and robot learning is now attempting the same for action.

## Cheat sheet & connections

| Name | Formula | Where |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Tabular cloning | $N(s, a) / \sum_{a'} N(s, a')$ | @eq-bc-counts |
| Hindsight relabelling | $-\sum \log \pi(a_t \mid s_t, g = s_{t'})$ | @eq-relabel |
| Bellman on the data ★ | $V(s) = \min_{(s,a,s') \in \mathcal{D}} [1 + V(s')]$ | @eq-bellman-data |
| Expert share | $N_e / (N_e + k M)$ | @eq-share |
| Ensemble disagreement | $\sum_j \operatorname{std}_m \pi_m(o)_j$ | @eq-disagreement |
| Acting selectively | act if $u \le \tau$; coverage, selective success | @eq-selective |
| CLIP loss ★ | softmax over rows and columns of $t\, X Y^\top$ | @eq-clip |
| SigLIP loss | $-\sum \log \sigma(z_{ij}(t\, x_i \cdot y_j + b)) / n$ | @eq-siglip |
| Hard negative | $1 - (1 - f)^{n - 1}$ | @eq-hardneg |
| Video memory | seconds × fps × tokens per frame | @eq-memory |

**Common pitfalls.**

- Mistaking a demo trained and tested in one room for generalization.
- Treating low model uncertainty as safety: it only measures familiarity of the input.
- Cloning a robot's own rollouts together with expert data, with equal weight.
- Throwing away failed rollouts: offline RL learns from them.
- Assuming more data alone helps: the guest's 10× data without a bigger model and longer training made things worse.
- Trusting a benchmark you have not looked at.
- Filtering web data towards one culture's images and losing the rest of the world.
- Freezing the image encoder inside a VLM that must learn relations or locations.

